00:17:23.780 --> 00:17:27.410
So another interesting benefit
of the Google Cloud Platform

00:17:27.410 --> 00:17:30.950
Marketplace is actually
helping you run open source.

00:17:30.950 --> 00:17:33.380
And we've actually made a
pretty substantial investment

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:34.850
over the years in
this program we

00:17:34.850 --> 00:17:36.620
called Google Click to Deploy.

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:38.884
Many of you may be using
these packages already,

00:17:38.884 --> 00:17:40.550
and we've made some
recent improvements.

00:17:40.550 --> 00:17:43.140
So I want to quickly talk
through what that looks like.

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:45.740
The spirit behind the
Click to Deploy program

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:48.770
is really accelerating your
use of open source in Cloud.

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:50.660
It's really that simple.

00:17:50.660 --> 00:17:54.560
The way we do that is we take
standard open source products,

00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:59.240
and we package them in ways that
you would have done yourself.

00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:01.350
So these are vanilla
applications.

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:03.650
We intentionally
do things in a way

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:06.050
where you can continue
following blog posts,

00:18:06.050 --> 00:18:08.030
stack overflow
tutorials, et cetera.

00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:09.710
We don't tamper the packages.

00:18:09.710 --> 00:18:12.350
What we do do is make sure
that they are optimized and run

00:18:12.350 --> 00:18:13.517
well on GCP.

00:18:13.517 --> 00:18:15.350
So we kind of have that
trust that if you're

00:18:15.350 --> 00:18:19.160
deploying Django,
Ruby, or any of these,

00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:22.790
you're going to be in a known
good state from the start.

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:25.700
Vanilla configurations,
as I mentioned.

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:28.120
They continue to be
community-supported.

00:18:28.120 --> 00:18:30.290
And so you can follow
in any of the tutorials,

00:18:30.290 --> 00:18:32.690
blogs that you might
have out there,

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:35.120
or work with any of the
vendors that offer support

00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:37.490
on top of open
source applications.

00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:40.550
Many of these solutions are
actually packaged in a way

00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:42.950
that you can actually run them
in production really well.

00:18:42.950 --> 00:18:45.650
So it's one thing to run
a single-node LAMP stack,

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:49.100
but it's another two run a
seven-node cluster MongoDB

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:49.670
instance.

00:18:49.670 --> 00:18:51.320
And if you've ever had to
configure something like that,

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:54.080
you know that that takes hours,
sometimes days of your time,

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:55.940
and getting it right
can be quite tricky.

00:18:55.940 --> 00:18:59.150
With our program here, you can
actually run a clustered Mongo,

00:18:59.150 --> 00:19:02.100
for example, in under
five minutes, so really,

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:05.990
really easy to get up and
started with open source.

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:07.580
We offer VM images.

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:10.470
We offer base container
images you can use as well.

00:19:10.470 --> 00:19:13.950
So if you're hosting in your
own container environment,

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:15.390
you can just use those.

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:18.630
We have Kubernetes applications
that we've added as well.

00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:21.270
So really big investment
from the open source side

00:19:21.270 --> 00:19:21.770
from Google.

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:26.270
For the general GCP
Marketplace, we're

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:28.910
also focused on making sure
that it's a great way for you

00:19:28.910 --> 00:19:30.320
to accelerate your prototyping.

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:32.028
So we talked about
some of these benefits

00:19:32.028 --> 00:19:33.780
of deploying a few clicks.

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:35.780
We have these smart
pre-configured settings,

00:19:35.780 --> 00:19:38.720
so you're always going
to start in a good state.

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:41.150
Each of our solutions
comes with really robust

00:19:41.150 --> 00:19:43.520
getting started
guides and tutorials.

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:47.510
One of the most frustrating
experiences we all relate to

00:19:47.510 --> 00:19:50.476
is trying something out and
then just being stuck and not

00:19:50.476 --> 00:19:51.475
knowing what to do next.

00:19:51.475 --> 00:19:53.930
So that's kind of the thing
with all of our solutions,

00:19:53.930 --> 00:19:56.900
whether it's from Google or
from a third-party provider,

00:19:56.900 --> 00:19:59.630
really robust tutorials
getting started guides,

00:19:59.630 --> 00:20:02.512
making sure you don't get stuck.

00:20:02.512 --> 00:20:03.470
Again, really low risk.

00:20:03.470 --> 00:20:04.837
We talked about trials.

00:20:04.837 --> 00:20:05.920
You can try solutions out.

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:08.440
We have trials ranging
from 14 days to 30 days,

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:12.010
depending on the vendor,
so really robust.

00:20:12.010 --> 00:20:14.150
You can actually apply
free trial credits.

00:20:14.150 --> 00:20:17.140
So if you're new to
GCP or evaluating GCP,

00:20:17.140 --> 00:20:19.810
you have that budget of $300.

00:20:19.810 --> 00:20:22.960
You can actually use that
budget towards any solution

00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:26.290
on our marketplace, so it could
even be a commercial solution.

00:20:26.290 --> 00:20:30.510
And you can, in addition to
that, use free trial solutions

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:33.710
while you're on a GCP free
trial if that makes sense.

00:20:33.710 --> 00:20:36.220
So really, really
low risk way to trial

00:20:36.220 --> 00:20:40.870
the platform and
check out new apps.

00:20:40.870 --> 00:20:42.370
So it's great for open source.

00:20:42.370 --> 00:20:43.630
It's great for prototyping.

00:20:43.630 --> 00:20:45.720
But it's also really
great for production.

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:48.940
So I want to talk to you briefly
about how we think about that.

00:20:48.940 --> 00:20:52.250
So the journey just doesn't
stop when you're prototyping.

00:20:52.250 --> 00:20:55.330
You want to have these multi-VM
cluster configurations that you

00:20:55.330 --> 00:20:57.077
can depend on in production.

00:20:57.077 --> 00:20:59.410
And that's what we've done,
whether it's our open source

00:20:59.410 --> 00:21:01.420
solutions through the
Click to Deploy program

00:21:01.420 --> 00:21:04.690
or whether it's through our
vendors, clustered applications

00:21:04.690 --> 00:21:08.060
that you can actually
take to production.

00:21:08.060 --> 00:21:11.411
We also offer a lot of flexible
pricing models, including BYOL.

00:21:11.411 --> 00:21:13.660
So if you have existing
relationships with the vendor,

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:15.460
you can tap into those.

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:17.646
Support integration--
one of the key things

00:21:17.646 --> 00:21:19.270
of going into production
is making sure

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:21.520
that the vendor is going
to have your support.

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:23.050
And all of our
marketplace vendors

00:21:23.050 --> 00:21:27.040
are contracted to offer
that support that they

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:29.800
list on their details page.

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:31.270
With Kubernetes, you actually--

00:21:31.270 --> 00:21:32.230
I've written it as one line.

00:21:32.230 --> 00:21:33.780
But actually, the
power of Kubernetes

00:21:33.780 --> 00:21:35.140
is quite strong here.

00:21:35.140 --> 00:21:37.090
With Kubernetes solutions,
you can actually

00:21:37.090 --> 00:21:41.770
take the commercial application,
bring it down, open it up,

00:21:41.770 --> 00:21:43.632
customize it, and redeploy it.

00:21:43.632 --> 00:21:45.590
Again, you still get
metered, still get billed.

00:21:45.590 --> 00:21:47.800
You get one single
bill from Google,

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:49.930
and with the power
of Kubernetes,

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:52.600
you get some of the additional
benefits, like auto-healing.

00:21:52.600 --> 00:21:55.720
So if one particular
instance or node goes down,

00:21:55.720 --> 00:21:58.810
it'll quickly come back up
just because Kubernetes has

00:21:58.810 --> 00:22:01.900
that capability built for you.

00:22:01.900 --> 00:22:03.760
Another big focus
for us is making

00:22:03.760 --> 00:22:05.047
sure our solutions are secure.

00:22:05.047 --> 00:22:06.880
So we talked about the
verification pipeline

00:22:06.880 --> 00:22:09.190
that constantly runs
to make sure the latest

00:22:09.190 --> 00:22:11.080
vulnerabilities are detected.

00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:13.660
And we work very closely
with our vendors to make sure

00:22:13.660 --> 00:22:15.910
that the latest versions are
put up in the Marketplace

00:22:15.910 --> 00:22:16.743
as soon as possible.

00:22:19.540 --> 00:22:21.912
And in addition to all
of those, another thing

00:22:21.912 --> 00:22:23.620
you want to do in
production is make sure

00:22:23.620 --> 00:22:27.310
that you have a way to
tweak these configurations.

00:22:27.310 --> 00:22:29.600
You're not just getting
stock things from Google

